[[:db/cas 17592186106470 :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{3}{8})^{7}$?" "What is $\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)^{7}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186106471 :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186106471 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{2}=9$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{25}.\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{2}=9$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{25}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106472 :question/problem "$6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=$" "$6{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186106472 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=6\\cdot \\frac{8}{27}=\\frac{48}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{27}=\\frac{16\\cdot 3}{9\\cdot 3}=\\frac{16}{9}\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}=6\\cdot \\frac{8}{27}=\\frac{48}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{27}=\\frac{16\\cdot 3}{9\\cdot 3}=\\frac{16}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106473 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186106473 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(-1)}{7}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{7})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${7}^{2}=49,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{49}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(-1)}{7}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{(-1)}{7}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${7}^{2}=49,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{49}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106474 :question/problem "What is $(-\\frac{5}{4})^{3}$?" "What is $\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)^{3}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186478376 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186478376 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(-1)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(-1)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{(-1)}{5}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478377 :question/problem "${(\\frac{5}{8})}^{9}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{9}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186478377 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $9$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $9:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{9}=\\frac{{5}^{9}}{{8}^{9}}\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $9$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $9:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{9}=\\frac{{5}^{9}}{{8}^{9}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478380 :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186478380 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${4}^{3}=64,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{64}.\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${4}^{3}=64,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{64}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478425 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186478425 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{(-1)}{9}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${9}^{2}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{81}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{(-1)}{9}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{(-1)}{9}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${9}^{2}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{81}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493896 :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186493896 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536830 :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186536830 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${5}^{3}=125,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${5}^{3}=125,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544795 :question/problem "$10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=$" "$10{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186544795 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=10\\cdot \\frac{8}{125}=\\frac{80}{125}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{125}=\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25\\cdot 5}=\\frac{16}{25}\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n10{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3}=10\\cdot \\frac{8}{125}=\\frac{80}{125}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{125}=\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25\\cdot 5}=\\frac{16}{25}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544797 :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186544797 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${7}^{3}=343,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}=\\frac{27}{343}.\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${7}^{3}=343,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}=\\frac{27}{343}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544861 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186544861 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(-2)}{3}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}={(\\frac{(-2)}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{4}=16$ and ${3}^{4}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}=\\frac{16}{81}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(-2)}{3}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{4}={\\left(\\frac{(-2)}{3}\\right)}^{4}=\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{4}=16$ and ${3}^{4}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}=\\frac{16}{81}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 175921865765