d the denominator to the power of $5:$\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{9}\\right)^{5}=\\frac{{5}^{5}}{{9}^{5}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606794 :question/problem "$3{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3}=$" "$3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606794 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3}=\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{6}^{3}}=\\frac{125}{216}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3}=3\\cdot \\frac{125}{216}=\\frac{375}{216}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{375}{216}=\\frac{125\\cdot 3}{72\\cdot 3}=\\frac{125}{72}\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{6}^{3}}=\\frac{125}{216}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{3}=3\\cdot \\frac{125}{216}=\\frac{375}{216}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{375}{216}=\\frac{125\\cdot 3}{72\\cdot 3}=\\frac{125}{72}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606806 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606806 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(-2)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-2)}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{2}=4$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(-2)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{(-2)}{5}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{2}=4$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606820 :question/problem "${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606820 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}=\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${5}^{3}=125$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{125}{8}.\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${5}^{3}=125$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{125}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606838 :question/problem "$24{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}=$" "$24{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606838 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{64}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n24{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}=24\\cdot \\frac{9}{64}=\\frac{216}{64}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{216}{64}=\\frac{27\\cdot 8}{8\\cdot 8}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{64}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n24{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{2}=24\\cdot \\frac{9}{64}=\\frac{216}{64}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{216}{64}=\\frac{27\\cdot 8}{8\\cdot 8}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606856 :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{7})}^{8}=$" "${\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}^{8}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606856 :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $8$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $8:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{8}=\\frac{{3}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$" "To raise the fraction to the power of $8$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $8:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}^{8}=\\frac{{3}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606881 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606881 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(-3)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}={(\\frac{(-3)}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-3)^{3}=-27$ and ${5}^{3}=125,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{-27}{125}=-\\frac{27}{125}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(-3)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{3}={\\left(\\frac{(-3)}{5}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-3)^{3}=-27$ and ${5}^{3}=125,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{-27}{125}=-\\frac{27}{125}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186606894 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{4}=$" "${\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186606894 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(-1)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{4}={(\\frac{(-1)}{5})}^{4}=\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{{5}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{4}=1$ and ${5}^{4}=625,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{{5}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{625}.\n$$" "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(-1)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{4}={\\left(\\frac{(-1)}{5}\\right)}^{4}=\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{{5}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{4}=1$ and ${5}^{4}=625,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{{5}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{625}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107083 :tutorial/content "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3$ by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 2}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3$ by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3} & =\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 2}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186576549 :question-generator/script "generators/25/250aef5c6ab58938ad9a65f65f0c3035b19c545e1469ce12047c38a2483b6796/generator.py" "generators/b7/b7bd1af185f0af93e9a36f00496f3f84718095316224dc7d9f435d2fa86203f3/generator.py"] [:db/ensure 17592186106470 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186106471 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186106472 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186106473 :question/valid